      "title": "Funding Biotech",
      "description": "The biotechnology (biotech) industry is one of the most capital-intensive segments of the health care industry.  The actual cost to bring a drug from the research labs to patients is estimated to be in the hundreds of millions of dollars, and yet only 10% of all drugs that enter human testing are ever approved, often failing due safety issues or lackluster efficacy.  Therefore, when you factor in the cost of these drug failures, the cost to get a drug approved can reach into the billions.  And that’s not even considering the cost to market the drug once approved.  Despite these inherent clinical and commercial risks coupled with the high costs of developing and marketing a drug, there was over $500 billion of equity capital invested into biotech companies over the past 10 years signaling a robust market where returns are disproportionate to risk.\nThis course will explore funding these risky biotech ventures from the perspective of the biotech CEO.  Students will learn about the various ways biotech companies are capitalized (e.g., equity, convertible/structured debt, licensing partnerships, royalty monetization, etc.) and the tradeoffs a biotech CEO considers when deciding which options to pursue at various stages of the company’s evolution.  Towards the end of the course, we will ‘zoom out’ to better understand the geopolitical threats facing the U.S. biotech industry and what can be done to ensure the U.S. biotech industry continues to be a strategic national asset for the U.S. economy and, in turn, remain a beacon of hope for all key stakeholders, most importantly, the patient.  For the final project, students will analyze the capital requirements for a U.S. public biotech company and make capital acquisition and allocation recommendations to the company’s executive team based on the financing options available to the firm.\nThrough readings, lectures, interactive case study discussions, guest speakers, and a final project, students will learn concepts and analytical frameworks and acquire the tools and skills necessary to make important capital allocation decisions as a biotech CEO.",
